      {
        "id": 151399,
        "name": "Trajector",
        "username": "Trajector",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/{size}/1914_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-12-29T16:18:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>At some point, they just have to stay within the confines of the game they defined. Part of the great thing about Sentinels is that - even without Vengeance style - it allows tremendous variety within its set format. The more any particular villain deviates from that format, the more that villain might as well just be part of a different game entirely. So I don’t see the final villain requiring tons of heroes or anything like that.</p>\n<p>EDIT: In the interest of contributing something more substantive and fun to the discussion, what about a villain that acts like a more extreme version of Plague Rat? Say, the villain deck has a few of these in it:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>TURN<br>\nOngoing<br>\nWhen this card enters play, select a player through some largely random mechanism.</p>\n<p>Treat all of that player’s cards as if they have the keyword Villain.</p>\n<p>Treat all other players’ cards as Hero cards.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That player would then face a dilemma…continue to help the heroes, even as they take damage as part of “all villain targets,” or try to force a condition that causes the heroes to lose, thus winning themselves.</p>\n<p>I will call this hypothetical villain “Palpatine.”</p>",
        "post_number": 36,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:30:14.708Z",
